# -*- mode:org; coding:utf-8 -*-
#+TITLE: dribble-eof-prompt-recorded — the prompt pending at EOF lands in the dribble as ";; O: _$"
#+STATUS: DONE
#+PRIORITY: P3
#+TYPE: bug
#+DEPENDS: -
#+BRANCH: fix-dribble-eof-prompt

* Symptom

Every dribble file that is still open when the session ends gains a
trailing line:

: ;; O: _$

=dribble.issue= states the opposite rule — "prompts are not recorded: a
partial output line still open when an input line completes is prompt
text and is dropped, not emitted."

* Repro (clautolisp 1.8.28)

: printf '(+ 1 1)\n' | clautolisp-sbcl --dribble=/tmp/t.log
: cat /tmp/t.log

Last line is =;; O: _$=. It does *not* appear when the dribble is
closed explicitly with =(clal-dribble)= before exit, because the input
echo has just discarded the pending partial line at that point.

* Cause

=DRIBBLE-STOP= (=clautolisp/tools/clautolisp/source/dribble.lisp=)
calls =(%dribble-terminate-open-line)= with no =:discard=, so a partial
line pending at stop time is emitted rather than dropped. At EOF the
pending partial is the prompt that was printed while waiting for input
that never came.

* Why this is NOT simply "pass :discard t"

The suppression rule elsewhere is evidence-based: the input echo
discards a pending partial *because an input line completed*, which
proves the partial was a prompt. At EOF there is no such proof — a
program ending with =(princ "no trailing newline")= leaves a pending
partial that is genuine output and must be recorded. Discarding
unconditionally at stop would silently drop it.

So the current behaviour is the conservative half of a real tradeoff,
not an oversight: it keeps real output at the cost of one spurious
prompt line. Anyone "fixing" this must preserve unterminated genuine
output.

* Proposal (if judged worth doing at all)

Have the REPL tell the dribble that what it is about to print is a
prompt — e.g. emit prompts through a distinguishable path, or set a
flag around the prompt write — so =DRIBBLE-STOP= can discard a pending
partial *known* to be a prompt and emit anything else. That is the only
way to satisfy both rules at once.

Low priority: one cosmetic line at the end of a file, and the
neighbouring =dribble-stop-announcement-interactor.issue= is in the
same "known, minor, tradeoff" category. WONTFIX is a defensible
outcome; the ticket exists so the next reader does not mistake it for
an unnoticed bug.

* Notes

Found 2026-08-11 while verifying dribble.issue end-to-end for closure.

* DONE — 2026-08-19 (clautolisp 1.8.53)

Done as the Proposal describes: the writer says what it is writing, so
the two rules stop being in conflict.

** The mechanism

=CLAUTOLISP.INTERACTOR:*WRITING-PROMPT*= is bound by INTERACTOR-LOOP
around the prompt it prints, and by =WITH-DRIBBLE-PROMPT= around the
REPL reader's own prompt writes. The tee tracks whether the open line is
PURELY prompt text — one non-prompt character clears it, because a line
that is partly real output is real output.

The variable lives in the interactor package, not in the dribble: which
characters are a prompt is knowledge only the loop has. Downstream a
prompt is indistinguishable from a =princ= that ends a program without a
newline. The dribble is one consumer of that fact, not its owner.

** What the first attempt got wrong, and what it cost

The obvious placement — discard the pending partial at =DRIBBLE-STOP=,
now that it is known to be a prompt — DOES NOT WORK, and the repro above
still printed the trailing prompt line after it. Instrumenting
=DRIBBLE-STOP= showed why: by then there is no open line at all. The
prompt line had already been emitted, terminated by the INTERLEAVING
rule when another stream wrote. Deciding at stop time was deciding too
late.

So the decision sits in =%TEE-EMIT-BUFFERED-LINE=, the one place every
termination path passes through. That is also the more faithful reading
of the format rule: prompts are not recorded WHEREVER the line ends, not
only where the input echo happens to notice.

Worth recording, because the reasoning that produced the wrong placement
was sound and the code still failed. Only running this ticket's own
repro against a real built binary caught it.

** Verification — this ticket's repro, against a built binary

: ;; H: clautolisp 1.8.51
: (+ 1 1)
: ;; O: 2

No trailing prompt line. And the conservative half the ticket insists
on, preserved — the same session ending with an unterminated =princ=:

: ;; H: clautolisp 1.8.51
: (princ "no trailing newline")
: ;; O: no trailing newline"no trailing newline"

Four tests (92 checks green in the tool suite): the pending prompt
dropped, unterminated real output kept, a line that starts as a prompt
and continues as output kept, and — the regression guard for the wrong
placement above — a prompt terminated by an interleaved write dropped.
